# React Component Conventions

## Component Declaration

Use function declarations with explicit props interfaces:

```typescript
interface MetricCardProps {
  title: string;
  value: number;
  trend?: "up" | "down" | "flat";
  className?: string;
}

function MetricCard({ title, value, trend = "flat", className }: MetricCardProps) {
  return (
    <div className={cn("rounded-lg border p-4", className)}>
      <h3 className="text-sm font-medium text-muted-foreground">{title}</h3>
      <p className="text-2xl font-bold">{value.toLocaleString()}</p>
      {trend !== "flat" && <TrendIndicator direction={trend} />}
    </div>
  );
}

export { MetricCard };
```

## Server vs Client Components

- Components are server components by default. Do not add `"use client"` unless required.
- Add `"use client"` when the component uses: `useState`, `useEffect`, `useRef`, event handlers (`onClick`, `onChange`), browser APIs, or third-party client-only libraries.
- Keep client components as small and leaf-level as possible. Extract server-renderable parts upward.

## File Organization

```
src/components/
  ui/               -- shadcn/ui primitives (Button, Dialog, etc.)
  dashboard/        -- Dashboard feature components
    MetricCard.tsx
    MetricCard.test.tsx
    ChartPanel.tsx
    DashboardHeader.tsx
  settings/         -- Settings feature components
  shared/           -- Cross-feature components (PageHeader, EmptyState, etc.)
```

## Composition Over Configuration

Prefer composable components over prop-heavy ones:

```typescript
// Prefer this:
<Card>
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle>Revenue</CardTitle>
  </CardHeader>
  <CardContent>
    <LineChart data={revenueData} />
  </CardContent>
</Card>

// Over this:
<Card title="Revenue" chart={<LineChart data={revenueData} />} />
```

## Data Fetching

Fetch data in server components or use route handlers:

```typescript
// In a server component (page.tsx or layout.tsx)
async function DashboardPage() {
  const metrics = await getMetrics();
  return <MetricGrid metrics={metrics} />;
}
```

For client-side data that needs to update, use SWR or React Query with API routes:

```typescript
"use client";

import useSWR from "swr";

function LiveMetrics() {
  const { data, error, isLoading } = useSWR("/api/v1/metrics/live", fetcher, {
    refreshInterval: 30_000,
  });
  // ...
}
```

## Styling Rules

- Use Tailwind CSS classes exclusively. No CSS modules or inline styles.
- Use `cn()` from `@/lib/utils` for conditional classes.
- Use semantic color tokens: `text-primary`, `bg-muted`, `border-destructive`.
- Mobile-first responsive design: base styles for mobile, then `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`.

## Loading and Error States

Every async page segment should have corresponding files:

```
app/dashboard/
  page.tsx
  loading.tsx      -- Skeleton UI shown while page data loads
  error.tsx        -- Error boundary for this segment
```

Loading states should use skeleton components that match the layout:

```typescript
function DashboardLoading() {
  return (
    <div className="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
      {Array.from({ length: 3 }).map((_, i) => (
        <Skeleton key={i} className="h-32 rounded-lg" />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Accessibility

- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible.
- Use semantic HTML elements (`button`, `nav`, `main`, `section`).
- Images require meaningful `alt` text. Decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Use `aria-label` on icon-only buttons.
- Color is never the sole indicator of state (add icons or text).

## Testing

- Test user-visible behavior, not implementation details.
- Use Testing Library queries in priority order: `getByRole` > `getByLabelText` > `getByText` > `getByTestId`.
- Test files colocated: `MetricCard.test.tsx` next to `MetricCard.tsx`.
